+# Privacy design and third-party inventory
+
+Reviewed November 8, 2026. Scope: all published HTML, CSS, image assets,
+client-side code, package dependencies, and deployment configuration.
+
+## Findings and changes
+
+| Component | Data exposure | Decision |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Unsplash hero image | Previously exposed each visitor's IP and request metadata to images.unsplash.com | Downloaded the existing photo to assets/coffee.jpg; no visitor request to Unsplash remains |
+| Fonts and styles | No external font service | Keep system fonts; serve CSS locally |
+| Client scripts, analytics, ads, embeds | None present | Keep pages script-free; CSP blocks scripts, connections, frames, workers, and forms |
+| Cookies and browser storage | No application reads or writes | No identifiers, consent cookie, fingerprinting, or storage SDK added |
+| Forms, accounts, checkout | No implementation in this repository | Do not collect information; removed the nonfunctional GET logout control rather than imply session termination |
+| Hosting | Host necessarily receives connection metadata | Operator must configure minimal logs and retention; provider and retention are not established by this repository |
+| Runtime dependencies | No bundled third-party client dependencies | The existing start tool is development tooling only; production publishes an explicit static file allowlist |
+
+Original image source (build-time provenance only):
+https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1495474472287-4d71bcdd2085?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1350&q=80
+
+## Build and deploy
+
+Run `npm run build` with Node.js 18 or later. The build audits public source,
+rejects active content and external resource references, checks header/fallback
+policy consistency, and copies only approved public paths into `dist/`.
+The source audit is deliberately conservative and is not a general HTML sanitizer.
+Review new assets and markup even when it passes.
+
+`netlify.toml` publishes `dist/`; its `_headers` file configures actual HTTP response
+headers on Netlify. Other hosts must explicitly apply the same header rules.
+The existing `npm start` static server does not enforce this deployment contract.
+The HTML CSP and referrer metadata provide partial protection on generic hosts;
+framing, permissions, HSTS, and MIME protections require HTTP response headers.
+Serve HTML as text/html, CSS as text/css, and the photograph as image/jpeg.
+
+Use HTTPS and redirect HTTP at the hosting layer. HSTS starts at 300 seconds
+without includeSubDomains or preload; increase only after verifying production
+TLS and domain coverage. No CSP reporting endpoint is configured: there is no
+collector, and reports can themselves contain private URLs or code samples.
+Do not send reports to an unreviewed third party.
+
+Before publishing and after hosting changes, check real response headers and
+provider-injected analytics against this policy. No browser or live-host checks
+were performed as part of this change. Header configuration alone cannot disable
+provider access logs: the operator must disable unnecessary logging, exclude
+query strings, tokens and personal fields, limit access, and select/document the
+shortest operational retention period before collecting customer data.
+
+## Requirements for future features
+
+- Review every dependency, external origin, embed, and network destination before
+  changing CSP. Record purpose, fields exposed, processor, retention, and removal
+  plan here. Re-audit at each release and at least quarterly. Use consent-triggered
+  facades for optional embeds, including locally hosted thumbnails.
+- Collect only fields needed for a stated task; support guest ordering. Explain
+  purpose and retention beside each field. Separate optional marketing consent.
+  Do not repurpose transactional data or require device permissions for browsing.
+- Keep secrets and personal information out of URLs, logs, HTML, and browser
+  local/session storage. Prefer in-memory transient state. Use textContent for
+  text and avoid HTML string injection and fingerprinting APIs.
+- Accounts and transactions require a real backend: authorize each request,
+  validate input, rate-limit sensitive endpoints, encrypt transport and stored
+  sensitive records, restrict access, and implement access/correction/deletion
+  with a documented retention schedule. Do not claim these exist in this build.
+- Use server-managed `__Host-` session cookies with Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax,
+  Path=/ and no Domain. Logout must be a CSRF-protected POST that revokes the
+  server session and expires its cookie; return Cache-Control: no-store and
+  Clear-Site-Data: "cookies", "storage", "cache" on successful logout. Keep these
+  headers off ordinary public pages. Apply no-store to authenticated responses.
+- Revisit form-action, connect-src, Permissions-Policy, and opener policy only
+  for required integrations. Do not weaken all pages to enable one feature.
